A||||5||batboy||Rack ok, upper shock mount bad||||1||
Z||000000||batboy||01-22-2000||05:43 PM||||I mentioned in the steering rack thread that I thought my rack was bad. It had me puzzled because when I replaced the inner tie rods I checked it all out and the rack seemed ok. My mechanic friend told me to check the shocks. Well, not really believing this could be a bad shock, I just went out and checked again. The shock is all right, but the upper shock mount on the driver's side (which is where all the clatter and banging seemed to be coming from) is flexing pretty bad. I checked the other side and my other two Fiero next, there was no flex in those shock mounts. Looks like I have to take the inner fender well off to get to it. Anyone ever have this problem? Should I just have it welded all up solid again? Is this front upper shock mount replaceable, as in does it unbolt? I'm breathlessly awaiting your honorable advice.||||reg||1
Z||000001||lowCG||01-22-2000||07:29 PM||||Get new shocks,and don't weld anything that needs to pivot on your suspension,that'd break the rod right next to the weld.<BR>I've pressed these in and out before,but the part is not available by itself <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><BR>Bilsteins from a Toyota pickup('94 2WD),at $50 each,worked out great on my car in front;bolted on up on top,but required that a hole be drilled into the lower control arm,and weld added/ground to make a good seat for the shock bushing along with cutting off one of the ears where the original shock mounted.<BR>If the car were an antique Porsche,I probably wouldn't have done these things,but,since I see just as many of these cars parked in the fields and yards as I do driving,what the heck,it works better than ever.||||reg||1
Z||000002||batboy||01-22-2000||08:28 PM||||I didn't quite follow all that, LowCG. I hope we're talking about the same thing. What I'm seeing flexing like a weight lifter is that upper frame bracket that the top of the shock bolts onto, the shock itself is ok. This is on my 85 GT, not my Batmobile. I'd rather keep things stock on my GT for now.||||reg||1
Z||000003||lowCG||01-23-2000||09:52 AM||||Sounds like you need a welder,or to tighten some loose bolts up in there.||||reg||1
Z||000004||WGraffiKs||01-24-2000||04:52 PM||||BB,<BR>I know EXACTLY what you mean, one of the upper mount bolts sheered on my '86, (Passenger side front) and the flex stress caused the mount on the wheelwell to crack, until the wheelwell metal broke.  I haven't fixed it yet, but looking at it, I think I'll weld a bar on the backside (actually on top)of the wheelwell and weld the original shock mounts to it.  I think the stock wheelwell metal will be too thin to simply weld back together with no support.  No idea how easy it will be to remove the entire wheelwell to do this, tho.  Maybe that's why I'm procrastinating!  No, the shockmounts don't unbolt.  The backing nuts are tack-welded to the inner wheelwell.  Time to replace those Monroe Formula GT's with Koni's!  Anyone know the cost for a pair?<P>[This message has been edited by WGraffiKs (edited 01-24-2000).]<p>[This message has been edited by WGraffiKs (edited 01-24-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||batboy||02-20-2000||10:14 PM||||<B>Upper shock mount update.</B> Since it was sunny and got up to about 60 degrees today, I had to go outside and mess around on my Fieros. I pulled the plastic front fender well out of my 85 GT. The upper shock mount is fastened in at the top with one bolt and the rest appears to be factory welded(?). Well, that one bolt was loose as a goose. I retightened the bolt and the problem appears mostly solved. The flexing looks like it may of weakened the welds a little, so I drilled a 3/8" hole through the side and slapped another bolt onto it using those big fender washers to distribute the load a little better. Now it seems much more solid. I may eventually have to weld a new plate in, but this should last a few months anyway.<p>[This message has been edited by batboy (edited 02-21-2000).]||||reg||1
